            <ArticleTitle>Evaluation and management of abdominal lymphoceles after anterior lumbar spine surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E852-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/BRS.0b013e3181891791</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine the management of abdominal lymphoceles after anterior spine surgery and to review the existing literature on lymphoceles after access to the spine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Abdominal lymphoceles are a rare complication of anterior spinal approaches.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report and review of the literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In this article, we describe the management of 2 lymphoceles. In the first case, immediate operative drainage was required because of worsening abdominal pain and distention. In the second patient, conservative management was attempted, but the lymphocele ultimately persisted and required surgical intervention.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Lymphoceles are often difficult to distinguish from ureteral injury, cerebrospinal fluid-leaks and hematomas using conventional imaging techniques. Although surgery remains the gold standard for the treatment of lymphoceles, expectant observation may be reasonable in some situations.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
